doc.add_heading('Rating scale questions',2)
p('The paper makes a significant contribution to scholarship.','Verbatim rating statement')
p('The literature review was thorough given the objectives and content of the article.','Verbatim rating statement')
p('The supplied scale reads “Strongly Disagree”, “Disagree”, “Neutral”, “Agree”, and “Strongly Agree”. Checkmarks were supplied, but their column alignment was not retained in the pasted feedback, so no rating category is inferred. The substantive concerns underlying these statements are addressed below.')
item('R1.1','Scientific contribution and overall experimental rigor',
'''The manuscript presents an interesting integration of pressure-based traffic optimization with the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA), and the pressure-based energy formulation constitutes the principal scientific contribution of the work. The mathematical mapping from traffic pressure to an Ising Hamiltonian is elegant and internally consistent. However, the current experimental design does not yet provide sufficient evidence to conclude that the reported improvements arise specifically from the use of QAOA rather than from more general properties of global optimization. Several additional analyses and controls would substantially strengthen the manuscript and improve the scientific rigor of the conclusions.''',
'''The reviewer’s distinction between the pressure-based objective and the method used to search it is accepted. The revision separates Pressure-Based Energy Optimization from QAOA Global Optimization and evaluates both against broader traffic-control benchmarks. Relative to the original submission, the manuscript adds a classical global comparison, exact optimum diagnostics, 3×3 experiments, energy–traffic correlations, bootstrap intervals, effect sizes, and parameter and synthetic-noise sensitivity analyses.

The revised interpretation is conditional: QAOA can produce different closed-loop traffic trajectories, but it does not improve on exhaustive enumeration as an optimizer of the same instantaneous Hamiltonian. Neither computational quantum advantage nor a benefit unique to quantum sampling has been established.''',
'Introduction, pp. 7–9; Sections 2.9–2.10, pp. 36–45; Sections 3.4–3.9, pp. 61–71; Section 4.2, pp. 77–80; Section 5, pp. 80–83.',
'Resolve the classical-controller implementation mismatch described in R1.2 and replace remaining statements that the comparison “isolates the quantum contribution” with the narrower claim that it compares the implemented global search procedures. A classical stochastic sampling control would be needed to test whether the observed behavior is specific to QAOA.')
item('R1.2','Separate optimization scope from optimization method',
'''The most significant methodological concern is that the current comparison simultaneously changes two experimental variables. The classical controller performs local optimization, whereas the QAOA controller performs global optimization over the entire network-wide Ising objective. Consequently, the observed performance improvements cannot be uniquely attributed to the quantum optimization algorithm because optimization scope (local versus global) and optimization strategy (classical versus QAOA) change simultaneously. The present design therefore contains an important confounding variable. A scientifically stronger comparison would include a classical global optimizer that minimizes exactly the same network-wide Ising Hamiltonian as the QAOA controller. Appropriate benchmarks include exhaustive enumeration (for the current network), simulated annealing, tabu search, branch-and-bound, mixed-integer optimization, or another established global optimization algorithm. Such comparisons would isolate the incremental contribution of the QAOA search strategy itself rather than demonstrating only that global optimization outperforms local optimization.''',
'''A Classical Global controller has been added alongside Classical Local and QAOA Global. Section 2.9 defines the common objective, including pressure bias, switching strength λ = 10, and neighbor coupling J = 2. This provides a global classical comparison without changing the spatial scope of the optimization. Tables 14–17 report the traffic results, and Table 21 reports QAOA-minus-Classical-Global differences and uncertainty.

The current code, however, requires a correction to the manuscript’s description of this benchmark. In both grid_2x2/2_classical_global/a7.py and grid_3x3/2_classical_global/a7.py, the phase recommendation comes from quantum_decision in annealer_classical.py. That routine performs stochastic simulated annealing with eight restarts and 512 iterations per restart. Exhaustive enumeration is then used to calculate an energy diagnostic, rather than to choose the applied recommendation. Thus, the workspace supports a matched-objective classical simulated-annealing comparison, but does not substantiate calling the deployed controller exact and deterministic.''',
'Section 2.1.4 and Table 1, p. 12; Sections 2.9.2–2.9.3, pp. 37–38; Section 2.9.8 and Table 11, p. 42; Tables 14–17, pp. 46, 49, 55 and 58; Section 3.6 and Table 21, pp. 67–68.',
'Either document the exact code version that generated the reported exact-controller results, or run a controller that actually applies an enumerated minimizer with a stated tie-breaking rule and regenerate the affected results. If simulated annealing is retained, label it accurately and quantify its stochastic variability. Identical input definitions and demand do not mean that independent closed-loop controllers encounter identical evolving traffic states; reserve same-state comparisons for the decision-level energy benchmark.')
item('R1.3','Exhaustive energy benchmark on the four-intersection network',
'''This issue is particularly important because the current experiments are performed on a 2×2 traffic network comprising only four intersections, corresponding to only 16 possible global signal configurations. For a problem of this size, the global optimum can be obtained exactly through exhaustive classical enumeration. Consequently, the manuscript should report the exact minimum Ising energy obtained by exhaustive search and compare it directly with both the classical heuristic and the QAOA solution. This would establish (i) whether QAOA actually converges to the global optimum, (ii) the probability that QAOA recovers the true optimum across repeated stochastic runs, and (iii) the optimality gap when it does not. Without this benchmark it is difficult to assess the actual optimization performance of the proposed quantum algorithm. Furthermore, because exhaustive optimization is computationally trivial for this network, it represents the appropriate gold standard rather than merely another optional comparison.''',
'''The revision adds exhaustive enumeration of all 16 configurations for each evaluated four-intersection QAOA decision and all 512 configurations for the nine-intersection analysis. The diagnostic compares the QAOA recommendation with the minimum of the same instantaneous energy function, using ΔE = E_selected − E_min. It is separate from the deployed classical controller discussed in R1.2.

At α = 0.7, Table 18 reports mean exact and QAOA energies of −92.131 and −92.057 on the 2×2 grid, with a mean gap of 0.074 and recovery of 99.40% (bootstrap 95% CI 99.30–99.50%). For 3×3, the corresponding values are −206.844, −183.520, a gap of 23.323, and recovery of 41.42% (39.85–43.09%). These are averages of run-level decision averages across changing Hamiltonians, rather than one fixed problem’s minimum. Recovery means selecting any energy-minimizing configuration, including tied minima; it is not a proof of convergence as circuit depth increases.''',
'Sections 2.9.3 and 2.9.7, pp. 37–38 and 41; Section 3.4 and Table 18, pp. 61–63; intended Figures 17–19.',
'Insert Figures 17–19. The current Table 18 directly compares QAOA with exact energy but does not supply a same-state Classical Local heuristic energy series. Add that diagnostic to complete the reviewer’s three-way energy comparison, and disclose the numerical optimum tolerance used in code (1e−9 for the recovery test) rather than implying exact floating-point equality.')
item('R1.4','Larger network evaluation',
'''The manuscript would also benefit from evaluating the methodology on a larger traffic network. While a 5×5 grid would certainly be informative, I believe that extending the study to at least a 3×3 grid would provide the most appropriate balance between computational feasibility and scientific value. A 3×3 network already increases the optimization problem substantially while remaining sufficiently tractable for comparison against strong classical optimization methods. Demonstrating that the proposed framework continues to provide benefits beyond the smallest non-trivial network would considerably strengthen the manuscript’s claims regarding practical applicability. If evaluation on a larger grid is beyond the intended scope of the current work, this limitation should at least be discussed explicitly.''',
'''The evaluation now includes a 3×3 grid with nine binary signal variables and 512 global configurations. Both the 600-second saturated α sweep and the 24-hour Seattle-derived demand experiment are evaluated on this larger network, with the same nine controller categories used for 2×2.

At α = 0.7, Table 15 reports throughput of 980 vehicles for QAOA, 944 for Classical Local, 940 for the controller labeled Classical Global, and 925 for Max-Pressure. QAOA waiting time is 43.75 s versus 47.16 s for Max-Pressure. The larger-grid results also expose limitations: exact-optimum recovery falls sharply, and some throughput gains accompany higher completed-vehicle delay. Section 5 explicitly limits generalization to small regular grids and distinguishes Seattle-derived demand from the actual Seattle road network.''',
'Sections 2.2–2.2.2, pp. 12–13; Sections 2.7.9–2.7.10, pp. 31–32; Table 15, p. 49; Sections 3.2–3.3, pp. 53–60; Table 17, p. 58; Section 5, pp. 81–83.',
'Retain the benchmark qualification in R1.2 and insert the intended scaling and Seattle figures. Describe 3×3 as a controlled extension, not evidence of city-scale feasibility.')
item('R1.5','Synthetic quantum noise',
'''Another important concern is the evaluation of QAOA under an entirely idealized quantum computational model. The current simulations assume perfect quantum operations and do not consider gate infidelity, measurement errors, decoherence, depolarizing noise, parameter uncertainty, circuit connectivity constraints, transpilation overhead, or other realistic characteristics of present-day quantum hardware. The issue is not that the algorithm is simulated rather than executed on physical hardware—simulation is entirely appropriate for methodological development—but rather that the manuscript provides no assessment of how sensitive the reported performance gains are to deviations from ideal quantum execution. The conclusions would therefore be strengthened considerably by a systematic sensitivity analysis in which synthetic quantum noise is introduced into the simulation. Examples include gate perturbations, measurement noise, depolarizing channels, phase-flip or bit-flip errors, or parameter uncertainty in the QAOA rotation angles. Such analyses would determine whether the reported improvements remain robust under realistic execution conditions or whether they depend critically upon idealized assumptions.''',
'''The revision adds three synthetic perturbation families on the 3×3 saturated network at α = 0.7: Gaussian angle uncertainty with standard deviations 0.025, 0.050 and 0.100 rad; depolarizing probabilities 0.5%, 1% and 2%; and symmetric readout bit-flip probabilities 1%, 2% and 5%. Table 23 reports traffic metrics, optimality gaps and recovery for 10 runs per noisy condition and a 20-run ideal reference. Its intervals are Student-t intervals, not bootstrap intervals.

The implementation in grid_3x3/3_quantum/annealer_noise.py perturbs γ and β, attaches depolarizing channels to modeled one- and two-qubit gates, and applies readout flips to sampled bits. Reported travel times span approximately 195.05–200.35 s under angle uncertainty, compared with 197.50 s for the ideal reference. Optimization quality changes more markedly and is not monotonic with noise. These findings support limited sensitivity statements for the tested perturbations; they do not establish hardware fault tolerance, equivalence of noisy and ideal performance, or persistence of every gain over classical baselines.''',
'Section 2.10.4, p. 45; Sections 3.8–3.9 and Table 23, pp. 70–71; Section 4.2.2, pp. 79–80; Section 5, pp. 82–83; intended Figures 25–26.',
'Insert the noise figures and merge the duplicate Sections 3.8 and 3.9. Expand the methods to state the perturbation placement, calibration procedure and seed handling. Retain explicit exclusions for device-calibrated decoherence, connectivity, latency and hardware overhead; overlapping intervals alone do not establish robustness by an equivalence test.')
item('R1.6','Hyperparameter and measurement sensitivity',
'''Related to this, the manuscript currently fixes the QAOA hyperparameters without demonstrating the robustness of the reported results. Performance should ideally be evaluated over a range of QAOA parameters (e.g., rotation angles, circuit depth, and measurement shots) to demonstrate that the observed improvements do not depend upon a particular parameter selection. Similarly, sensitivity analyses varying the number of measurement shots would provide insight into the stability of the optimization procedure.''',
'''The revision describes a 200-candidate calibration grid over γ, β and p, evaluated with 512 shots, and adds a separate one-at-a-time sensitivity experiment on 3×3 at α = 0.7. Table 22 varies γ over 4π/9, 7π/9 and π; β over π/9, π/6 and 5π/18; depth over 1, 2 and 3; and shots over 200, 512 and 1,000. The reference is γ = π, β = π/6, p = 2 and 512 shots, with 10 independent runs per setting.

Increasing depth from 1 to 3 raises exact recovery from 27.23% to 44.35% and decreases mean gap from 31.457 to 16.663. Increasing shots from 200 to 1,000 raises recovery from 27.63% to 48.13% and decreases gap from 32.319 to 19.231. Traffic outcomes are less sensitive and non-monotonic. The revision therefore separates improved approximation quality from improved traffic performance and acknowledges that the one-at-a-time design does not test parameter interactions.''',
'Sections 2.9.4–2.9.6, pp. 38–41; Section 3.7 and Table 22, pp. 68–69; Section 5, pp. 82–83; intended Figures 23–24.',
'Insert Figures 23–24 and specify the actual calibration frequency for each experiment rather than referring only to a schedule defined by the simulation mode. Table 22 uses Student-t intervals; avoid describing all sensitivity intervals as bootstrap estimates.')
item('R1.7','Relationship between Ising energy and traffic outcomes',
'''A further issue concerns the relationship between the Ising objective function and actual traffic performance. The manuscript implicitly assumes that minimizing the pressure-based Ising Hamiltonian necessarily translates into improved traffic metrics such as waiting time, travel time, or throughput. However, no analysis is presented demonstrating that lower Ising energies consistently correspond to improved network performance. It would therefore be valuable to quantify the relationship between optimized Ising energy and downstream traffic metrics through correlation or regression analyses. Such an analysis would validate the proposed objective function itself rather than merely the optimization algorithm applied to it.''',
'''Section 3.5 adds a within-condition correlation analysis of run-average optimality gap against travel time, waiting time and throughput. Variables are centered within each α condition to reduce confounding from different turning regimes. Each grid contributes 220 run records: 20 runs across 11 α values.

Table 19 reports 2×2 correlations of 0.29 for travel time, 0.28 for waiting time and −0.28 for throughput (all p < 0.001). On 3×3, travel time and waiting time show little association (r = −0.05, p = 0.458; r = −0.03, p = 0.631), while throughput has a weak negative correlation (r = −0.15, p = 0.032). The evidence therefore supports modest alignment on the smaller grid and a weaker relationship on the larger grid. It does not support the original implication that minimizing instantaneous energy necessarily minimizes future delay.

The analysis measures approximation gap, not the causal effect of changing absolute energy while holding traffic state fixed. The saved records summarize decisions over whole runs; these are run-level associations rather than time-lagged, decision-level predictions.''',
'Section 3.5 and Table 19, pp. 63–64; Sections 4.1.2 and 4.2, pp. 75–80; Section 5, pp. 81–83; intended Figure 20.',
'Insert Figure 20. Describe the findings as correlations with optimality gap, and avoid causal language or universal objective validation. Clarify whether the exploratory correlation p-values are adjusted for multiple testing.')
item('R1.8','Bootstrap intervals effect sizes and distributions',
'''The statistical analysis should also be strengthened. At present the manuscript relies primarily upon averages, standard deviations, and significance testing across repeated simulation runs. Given that QAOA is itself a stochastic optimization algorithm, I recommend reporting bootstrap confidence intervals for all principal outcome measures, including waiting time, travel time, throughput, and energy reduction. Confidence intervals on the improvement attributable to QAOA would provide substantially more information than p-values alone. In addition, reporting effect sizes (e.g., Cohen’s d, Hedges’ g, or appropriate non-parametric alternatives) would allow readers to assess the practical significance of the observed improvements rather than relying exclusively on statistical significance. Distributional visualizations such as boxplots or violin plots would further illustrate the variability of the optimization outcomes.''',
'''Tables 14–15 and 20 add percentile-bootstrap 95% intervals for the principal saturated traffic outcomes. Table 21 adds native-unit QAOA-minus-Classical-Global differences, bootstrap intervals, and one-sample Hedges’ g, including full-day Seattle outcomes. The table-generation script uses 10,000 run-level resamples; the standardized effect divides the mean difference by QAOA’s run-level sample standard deviation with a small-sample correction. This is not a pooled two-group effect size.

For illustration, Table 21 reports a 3×3 α = 0.7 throughput difference of +40 vehicles (95% CI 27–54; g = 1.22), while the travel-time difference is +1.03 s (−1.60 to 3.41) and waiting-time difference +0.21 s (−0.14 to 0.51). At α = 0.5, the throughput difference is +122 vehicles but travel time increases by 13.31 s. The manuscript therefore reports tradeoffs rather than selecting only favorable outcomes. Intended Figures 18 and 21 show run-average gap and traffic-outcome distributions.''',
'Tables 14–15, pp. 46 and 49; Section 3.6, Tables 20–21, pp. 64–68; intended Figures 18, 21 and 22.',
'The request is only partially complete: add bootstrap intervals for energy reduction, which is recorded in the code but not reported with bootstrap uncertainty in these tables. Resolve the Section 2.10.3 claim of 50 2×2 runs: the current JSON files and tables use 20 per α for both grids. Insert the distribution figures. Uncertainty relative to a deterministic classical benchmark is valid only once R1.2 is resolved; otherwise repeat and include the classical stochastic runs. These intervals condition on the fixed demand scenario and do not measure variation across independent traffic-demand realizations or RL training seeds.')
item('R1.9','Probability of optimum recovery',
'''Because the exact global optimum is known for the current network, an especially informative statistical measure would be the probability that QAOA recovers the global optimum, together with bootstrap confidence intervals for this probability. Alternatively, the manuscript could report the distribution of the optimality gap between QAOA and the exact optimum across repeated runs. These metrics would directly evaluate the optimization algorithm itself rather than relying solely on downstream traffic metrics.''',
'''Both diagnostics have been added. Section 3.4.2 describes the distribution of run-average gaps, and Section 3.4.3 reports optimum recovery with bootstrap intervals. At α = 0.7, recovery is 99.40% (99.30–99.50%) for 2×2 and 41.42% (39.85–43.09%) for 3×3. The median run-average gaps are 0.067 and 24.078, respectively.

Each run’s recovery fraction is calculated from its optimum-hit count divided by its number of evaluated decisions. Bootstrap resampling is across independent run records, preserving the within-run dependence of sequential decisions. These are decision-recovery fractions averaged across runs, not the probability that every decision in a simulation is optimal, nor the probability that an individual measurement shot returns an optimum.''',
'Section 2.9.7, p. 41; Sections 3.4.2–3.4.3 and Table 18, pp. 61–63; intended Figures 18–19.',
'Insert the figures and retain the run-level unit of analysis in their captions. Do not describe the stored run-average gap distributions as distributions of individual decision gaps.')
item('R1.10','Established adaptive traffic controllers',
'''Finally, the manuscript would benefit from broader benchmarking against established adaptive traffic-signal control methods. Although the current comparisons against fixed-time, queue-based control, and the proposed pressure-based controller are useful, additional comparisons with established adaptive controllers such as Max-Pressure, SCOOT, SCATS, PressLight, CoLight, MPLight, or other state-of-the-art adaptive traffic signal algorithms would place the proposed methodology within the context of the existing literature and provide a clearer indication of its practical significance.''',
'''The revised comparison includes Fixed-Time, Classical Local, Classical Global, QAOA Global, Max-Pressure, SCOOT-inspired control, PressLight, MPLight and CoLight. Sections 2.4–2.6 explain the added adaptive controllers and their implementation. The RL controllers are adaptations to the common two-phase action space, trained on 2×2 at α = 0.7 and then evaluated with frozen policies and exploration disabled. The SCOOT implementation is explicitly described as inspired by published principles rather than a certified commercial deployment. SCATS was not evaluated.

The expanded results show that QAOA does not dominate every method or metric. For example, at 3×3 α = 1.0, Table 15 reports Max-Pressure throughput of 1,096 versus 1,015 for QAOA. The discussion also recognizes strong delay results from CoLight and MPLight and the performance of cycle-based control in the daily scenario. These comparisons place the proposed formulation in a broader context while retaining implementation and training-transfer limitations.''',
'Sections 2.4–2.6, pp. 17–24; Section 2.10.3, pp. 44–45; Tables 14–17, pp. 46, 49, 55 and 58; Sections 4–5, pp. 73–83.',
'Use “SCOOT-inspired” consistently in tables and claims, retain the adaptation and frozen-policy qualifications, and avoid implying a comprehensive evaluation of commercial or fully tuned state-of-the-art systems. Correct the abstract’s inconsistent count: the listed total is nine controllers.')
item('R1.11','Overall assessment and requested strengthening',
'''Overall, I believe the proposed pressure-based Ising formulation is scientifically interesting and the manuscript has considerable potential. However, the current evidence does not yet isolate the contribution of the quantum optimization algorithm from the broader benefits of global optimization, nor does it quantify the robustness of the reported improvements to realistic quantum uncertainties. Incorporating an exact classical global optimization benchmark, extending the evaluation beyond the current 2×2 network, performing sensitivity analyses under synthetic quantum noise, strengthening the statistical analyses with bootstrap confidence intervals and effect sizes, evaluating the probability of recovering the true optimum, and validating the relationship between Ising energy and traffic performance would substantially increase the rigor, credibility, and long-term impact of the study.''',
'''The revision addresses the requested directions through the additions detailed in R1.2–R1.10: global classical comparison and exact energy diagnostics, the 3×3 evaluation, synthetic perturbation and hyperparameter tests, run-level statistical uncertainty and effects, optimum-recovery analysis, and energy-gap correlations. The discussion now separates the usefulness of the energy objective from the quality of its approximate optimization and states that the observed traffic advantages are conditional.

The central unresolved issue is the difference between an exact energy diagnostic and an exact controller. Resolving that implementation and provenance issue, completing the missing figures and energy-reduction uncertainty, and narrowing quantum-specific attribution are necessary before a complete response to this overall assessment can be claimed.''',
'Sections 2.9–2.10, pp. 36–45; Sections 3.2–3.9, pp. 53–71; Sections 4.2–5, pp. 77–83.',
'Complete the outstanding items in R1.2, R1.3 and R1.8 before representing all requested controls and statistical analyses as finished.')
