p('The paper makes a significant contribution to scholarship.','Verbatim rating statement')
p('The literature review was thorough given the objectives and content of the article.','Verbatim rating statement')
p('The same five-point scale and checkmark-alignment limitation described for Reviewer 1 apply. The responses below address the reviewer’s six open-response comments.')
item('R2.1','Reference links and citation accuracy',
'''Please verify that all links to the references point to the correct source.''',
'''The revised reference list includes 19 embedded external links, using DOI, publisher, repository or official-data destinations. The reference audit identifies several destinations that match their cited works, including the original SCOOT report, the AAAI “Toward a Thousand Lights” paper, the two arXiv algorithm papers, the United Nations urbanization report and the Frontiers Ising-formulation paper.

Full compliance cannot yet be claimed. Reference [1] links to the correct 2025 Urban Mobility Report but its author list is inconsistent with the linked report: the report lists David Schrank, Luke Albert, Kartikeya Jha and Bill Eisele, whereas the manuscript lists David Schrank, Bill Eisele and Tim Lomax. Reference [14] links to the project’s GitHub CSV rather than the original City of Seattle dataset. Several publisher links could not be fully inspected because of access or retrieval restrictions; that does not establish that they are broken. Section 2.6 also contains unresolved “(?)” citations.''',
'Section 2.6, pp. 23–24; References [1]–[19], pp. 84–86.',
'Correct Reference [1] metadata against the linked report, identify the original source and provenance of Reference [14] while retaining the repository copy as a reproducibility link, resolve the four “(?)” citations, and finish title-author-year checks for all remaining links in the final compiled PDF. Do not state that all links were verified until these checks pass. Verification source for [1]: https://static.tti.tamu.edu/tti.tamu.edu/documents/mobility-report-2025.pdf (title page).')
item('R2.2','Disclosure of preparation assistance',
'''The authors must disclose and acknowledge any assistance received in the preparation of this manuscript, including but not limited to editorial, technical, analytical, or writing support. All such contributions must be clearly stated in the Acknowledgments section.''',
'''Section 6 now acknowledges Eleftheria Fassman for guidance and feedback, Nancy Yuan and Kashif Charania for feedback and edits, Grammarly for grammar and spelling assistance, and Overleaf for manuscript preparation and formatting. It also states that Arnav Jha takes responsibility for the accuracy and integrity of the work.

The acknowledgment should describe actual assistance precisely and should not imply that use of an editor alone verified bibliographic correctness. The author should confirm names, affiliations and roles, and ensure that editorial, coding, analytical or writing assistance beyond the listed contributions is also disclosed. OpenAI Codex assisted with drafting and checking this response document against the supplied manuscript and project files; any use of that assistance in preparing the revised manuscript should be described accurately as well.''',
'Section 6 Acknowledgments, p. 83.',
'Confirm the complete assistance history and update the acknowledgment before asserting that disclosure is complete. Suggested addition, if adopted and verified by the author: “OpenAI Codex assisted in preparing the point-by-point response to reviewers and checking its consistency with the manuscript and project files. The author reviewed and verified the resulting response and retained responsibility for its final content.” Do not use this sentence to imply disclosure of any broader assistance history that has not been confirmed.')
item('R2.3','Recent and foundational literature',
'''Include enough recent references along with foundational ones. Ensure references directly support your claims. Avoid “padding.” Use credible sources (peer-reviewed journals, reputable books, official reports).''',
'''The revised Introduction combines foundational traffic and optimization literature with more recent work. Foundational sources include Max-Pressure (2013), SCOOT (1981), the Ising mapping (2014), QAOA (2014), and the original PressLight, MPLight and CoLight publications. Recent entries include a 2024 adaptive traffic-control review, 2025 urbanization and mobility reports, and 2025 quantum transportation literature. Sections 2.4–2.6 connect the adaptive-control citations to the implemented benchmarks.

Reference [11], “Hybrid quantum optimization in the context of minimizing traffic congestion,” is linked to an arXiv preprint and should be identified as such rather than presented as a peer-reviewed article. The literature discussion should distinguish quantum annealing from gate-based QAOA and relate each citation to the specific claim it supports. The revised scope does not require an arbitrary reference count.''',
'Introduction, pp. 5–9; Sections 2.4–2.6, pp. 17–24; Sections 2.8–2.9, pp. 33–41; References, pp. 84–86.',
'Resolve the unsupported SCOOT citation markers, complete the reference metadata audit in R2.1, and review strong claims such as the “urban arterial standards” assertion on p. 30 for direct source support. Preprint status source: https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.08275.')
item('R2.4','Explicit assumptions justification and impact',
'''All assumptions (including implicit assumptions) must be explicitly stated and clearly justified. The authors should explain why each assumption is reasonable and discuss its impact on the results and conclusions.''',
'''The revision makes several modeling choices explicit: regular 2×2 and 3×3 grids and binary NS/EW decisions (Sections 2.2 and 2.9.1); queue and pressure weights (Section 2.3); Seattle count filtering and spatial assignment (Section 2.7); equal left/right shares of the residual turning probability and route limits (Sections 2.7.7–2.7.9); fixed energy weights (Sections 2.8–2.9); frozen transferred RL policies (Section 2.5); and a finite 600-second evaluation horizon with completed-vehicle outcomes (Section 2.10). Section 5 discusses the consequences of regular geometry, small network size, fixed weights, simplified Seattle mapping and simulated quantum execution.

These assumptions serve tractability and controlled comparison, but restrict external validity. Binary phases omit richer intersection movements; symmetric turning and route limits simplify demand; fixed weights can be poorly matched to low demand or larger grids; and completed-vehicle averages can change when controllers clear different subsets of vehicles. The correlation analysis now directly tests, and limits, the assumption that better instantaneous energy optimization implies better downstream traffic.''',
'Sections 2.2–2.3, pp. 12–17; Section 2.5, pp. 18–22; Section 2.7, pp. 25–32; Sections 2.8–2.10, pp. 33–45; Section 3.5, pp. 63–64; Section 5, pp. 81–83.',
'A claim that all assumptions are fully justified would be premature. Add an explicit assumption–rationale–impact account covering perfect TraCI state access, fixed-demand conditional inference, route truncation, controller timing and tie-breaking, RL training transfer, parameter calibration frequency, and unmodeled quantum latency. Correct the false determinism assumption in R1.2. Clearly distinguish recommendations from phases actually executed after minimum-green and clearance constraints.')
item('R2.5','Conclusions supported by the evidence',
'''Avoid overreaching conclusions that extend beyond what is supported by the data and analysis.''',
'''Sections 4 and 5 now emphasize conditional performance and acknowledge that no controller dominates all scenarios or metrics. The revision states that QAOA cannot outperform exhaustive enumeration on the shared instantaneous Hamiltonian, that the 3×3 network remains small, that energy–traffic alignment weakens with network size, and that synthetic-noise results are not hardware validation. It also reports unfavorable outcomes and throughput–delay tradeoffs.

The appropriate conclusion is that the tested pressure-based framework and QAOA sampling procedure produced regime-dependent simulated traffic outcomes. The experiments do not establish quantum computational speedup, uniqueness of the sampling benefit to quantum methods, city-scale deployment readiness, or universal superiority to adaptive control.''',
'Section 3.5, pp. 63–64; Section 3.6, pp. 67–68; Sections 4.2.1–4.2.2, pp. 77–80; Section 5, pp. 80–83.',
'Remove remaining overstatements, including “isolates the quantum contribution” in the abstract on p. 4 and “the use of a quantum annealer still has benefits” on p. 81. Use “simulated gate-based QAOA” consistently for this implementation. Qualify causal explanations of stochasticity as hypotheses unless directly tested, and reconcile all exact-classical claims with R1.2.')
item('R2.6','Tense and third person',
'''Verify that you have used past perfect tence and third person throughout the manuscript wherever applicable.''',
'''The manuscript generally uses third-person descriptions of the study, controllers and author. However, the supplied revision still mixes present and past tense, and a completed manuscript-wide language check cannot yet be certified. Past perfect is appropriate when one past event preceded another; simple past remains appropriate for completed experimental procedures and results, while present tense can describe equations and established facts. Applying past perfect mechanically to every sentence would obscure the chronology.

The final edit should use third person consistently and past perfect where the sequence requires it, for example: “The policies had been trained on the 2×2 network before they were frozen for evaluation.” Completed results can be stated as “QAOA completed 980 vehicles on average.” This respects the reviewer’s “wherever applicable” qualification while maintaining clear scientific prose.''',
'Manuscript-wide; particularly Section 2.5, p. 19; Section 2.10.3, pp. 44–45; Sections 3–5, pp. 46–83.',
'Complete the language edit, retain the reviewer’s original spelling “tence” only in the verbatim quotation, remove the duplicate abstract on pp. 1–5, and correct duplicate headings and informal phrasing. Update this response to describe the completed language check only after the manuscript itself has been edited.')
doc.add_heading('Author verification before submission',1)
p('The responses above can be finalized once the following substantive and editorial discrepancies have been resolved. This section is for the author and should be removed from the submission copy after the checks are complete.')
checks=[
('Classical benchmark provenance','The active a7.py scripts in both classical-global directories import and call simulated annealing; the subsequent exhaustive routine measures the gap. Confirm the source revision behind the saved results or regenerate a genuine exact-controller benchmark. This affects the deterministic-baseline description, confidence intervals, effect sizes and quantum-attribution statements.'),
('Circuit and energy convention','Check the cost-unitary derivation against the implementation before claiming mathematical identity. With the stated spin mapping s = 2x − 1, computational-basis Z has eigenvalue −s. The scoring energy −h·s − J·s_i·s_j therefore corresponds to +h·Z − J·Z_i·Z_j. The implemented positive 2γJ RZ in the CNOT–RZ–CNOT block corresponds to +J·Z_i·Z_j under the stated exp(−iγH) convention. Reconcile the coupling sign and the omitted J in manuscript Equation 50 with an explicit diagonal-unitary check; any correction affecting generated samples requires reassessing results.'),
('Missing evidence presentation','The PDF contains intended-figure placeholders, including Figures 17–26 used to answer the reviewer. Insert and verify the actual plots. Add the missing bootstrap energy-reduction results and the requested local-heuristic energy comparison.'),
('Statistical reporting','Section 2.10.3 says 50 2×2 runs, whereas the saved analysis data contain 20 runs per α and the tables use 20. Reconcile the provenance and count. Preserve the distinction between bootstrap intervals in Tables 18, 20 and 21 and Student-t intervals in Tables 22 and 23. Explain fixed-demand and frozen-policy uncertainty limits.'),
('References and acknowledgments','Correct the 2025 mobility-report authors; resolve the four missing SCOOT citations; complete link verification; identify the original Seattle source behind the GitHub CSV; confirm all assistance disclosures, including use of Codex in the response preparation.'),
('Manuscript cleanup','Remove the duplicate abstracts and noise headings, correct the controller count, insert figures, narrow residual quantum-advantage language, and perform the requested tense and third-person review. Refresh all response page, section, table and figure references after final compilation.')]
for title,txt in checks:
    doc.add_heading(title,2);p(txt)
